<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="chp8sec2" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Elementary Matrix Decompositions (EMDs)</title>

    <definition xml:id="def-emd">
      <title>Elementary Matrix Decomposition</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          If <m>A</m> is a square matrix, then an <alert>elementary matrix decomposition</alert> of <m>A</m> is an expression <m>A=F_1F_2\cdots F_r</m> where each <m>F_i</m> is an elementary matrix.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
      Since elementary matrices are associated to stretches, shears, and reflections, an elementary matrix decomposition allows us to understand <m>T_A</m> as a composition of these simple transformations. The complexity comes in the fact that there will usually be many elementary matrices in such a decomposition.
    </p>

    <p>
      Given such a decomposition, I expect that you are able to correctly verbally interpret such a decomposition using geometric words, clearly and correctly identifying the order of the transformations. See, for example,  <xref ref="activity-ch2-composition" />.
    </p>

    <p>
      Now the question becomes, how do we <em>construct</em> such a decomposition. Here is a simple yet powerful observation:
    </p>

    <p>
      <ol>
        <li><p><em>If there is a sequence of elementary row operations transforming matrix <m>A</m> into matrix <m>B</m>, (i.e. <m>A\sim B</m>),</em></p></li>
        <li><p><em>then there is a sequence of elementary matrices such that <m>E_r\cdots E_2E_1A=B</m>.</em></p></li>
        <li><p><em>Using the Socks and Shoes property (<xref ref="prop-socks_n_shoes" />) to move these elementary matrices to the other side, we get <m>A=E_1^{-1}\cdots E_r^{-1}B</m>.</em></p></li>
      </ol>
    </p>

    <p>
      By choosing different <m>B</m>'s and different sequences of row operations, we obtain different matrix decompositions of <m>A</m>!
    </p>

    <p>
      To begin, for any <m>m\times n</m> matrix <m>A</m>, let <m>B=\rref(A)</m> and choose any sequence of row operations. We then get a decomposition <m>A=E_1^{-1}\cdots E_r^{-1}\rref(A)</m>. In particular, if <m>m=n</m>, <m>\rref(A)=I</m>, and writing <m>F_i=E_i^{-1}</m>, then we get the following.
    </p>

    <insight xml:id="construction-emd">
      <title>Construction: Elementary Matrix Decompositions</title>
      <p>
        If <m>A</m> is an invertible <m>n\times n</m> matrix, then to construct an elementary matrix decomposition:
      </p>
      <p>
        <ol>
          <li><p>Row reduce <m>A</m> to <m>I</m>, recording each row operation,</p></li>
          <li><p>Write down the associated product <m>E_r\cdots E_2E_1A=I</m>,</p></li>
          <li><p>Socks and Shoes (<xref ref="prop-socks_n_shoes" />) these matrices over to get <m>A=E_1^{-1}\cdots E_r^{-1}I</m> (you can then drop the <m>I</m>).</p></li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </insight>

    <p>
      Observe that since the inverse of an elementary matrix is an elementary matrix, this is an elementary matrix decomposition. Also, since there are many sequences of row operations to reduce <m>A</m> to <m>I</m>, there are accordingly many different possible elementary matrix decompositions.
    </p>

    <p>
      I expect you should be able to correctly perform this process by hand for <m>2\times 2</m> and <m>3\times 3</m> matrices.
    </p>

    <example xml:id="ex-emd">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Consider the invertible matrix <m>A=\begin{bmatrix}2\amp 3\\1\amp 2\end{bmatrix}</m>. Row reduction gives
          <md>
			<mrow>
				\begin{bmatrix}2\amp 3\\1\amp 2\end{bmatrix} 
				\rowP{1}{2} 
				\begin{bmatrix}1\amp 2\\2\amp 3\end{bmatrix} 
				\rowA{2}{1}{-2}
				\begin{bmatrix}1\amp 2\\0\amp -1\end{bmatrix} 
			</mrow>
			<mrow>
				\rowS{2}{-1}
				\begin{bmatrix}1\amp 2\\0\amp 1\end{bmatrix} 
				\rowA{1}{2}{-2}
				\begin{bmatrix}1\amp 0\\0\amp 1\end{bmatrix}.
			</mrow>
          </md>
          Recording the row operations with elementary matrices gives
          <me>
            \begin{bmatrix}1\amp -2\\0\amp 1\end{bmatrix} \begin{bmatrix}1\amp 0\\0\amp -1\end{bmatrix} \begin{bmatrix}1\amp 0\\-2\amp 1\end{bmatrix} \begin{bmatrix}0\amp 1\\1\amp 0\end{bmatrix}A = I.
          </me>
          Lastly, Socks and Shoesing it over, and dropping the superfluous <m>I</m>, gives
          <me>
            A= \begin{bmatrix}0\amp 1\\1\amp 0\end{bmatrix} \begin{bmatrix}1\amp 0\\2\amp 1\end{bmatrix} \begin{bmatrix}1\amp 0\\0\amp -1\end{bmatrix} \begin{bmatrix}1\amp 2\\0\amp 1\end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
          When <m>A</m> is viewed as a transformation of the plane, this <em>means</em> that <m>A</m> can be viewed as a composition of the following transformations:
        </p>
        <p>
          <ol>
            <li><p>First, shear the vector <m>\begin{bmatrix}0\\1\end{bmatrix}</m> two units in the positive <m>x</m>-direction.</p></li>
            <li><p>Next, reflect through the <m>x</m>-axis.</p></li>
            <li><p>Next, shear the vector <m>\begin{bmatrix}1\\0\end{bmatrix}</m> two units in the positive <m>y</m>-direction.</p></li>
            <li><p>Last, reflect through the line <m>y=x</m>.</p></li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </example>

    <question xml:id="activity-emd-practice">
        <p>
          Compute an elementary matrix decomposition for each of the following:
          <me>
            \begin{bmatrix}3\amp 1\\1\amp 2\end{bmatrix} \hspace{2pc} \begin{bmatrix}3\amp 4\\2\amp 3\end{bmatrix} \hspace{2pc} \begin{bmatrix}2\amp 4\\1\amp 2\end{bmatrix}
          </me>
        </p>
    </question>

    <note xml:id="note-geogebra-emd">
      <p>
        See a visual interpretation of an elementary matrix decomposition for <m>\begin{bmatrix}3\amp 1\\1\amp 2\end{bmatrix}</m> at GeoGebra ID: <url href="https://www.geogebra.org/m/yfqdvzbk"><alert>yfqdvzbk</alert></url>.
      </p>
    </note>


</section>